.It Fl W , -raidz-parity-epochs-test
Perform a dedicated raidz parity-epoch test.
The pool gets one raidz vdev, and its parity-epoch table gets five epochs in
turn: the next parity in the cycle 1, 2, 3, the one after that, and the second
one again, so that blocks are laid out with a higher and with a lower parity
than the vdev's own, then two epochs of random parities narrower than the
vdev, the last one with a single data column, whose blocks span several rows.
In each epoch the test writes objects of many sizes, frees some of them in the
next epoch to leave gaps, allocates ZIL-sized blocks until the vdev is nearly
full, which takes in the gaps and the end of the metaslabs it fills, writes
blocks that are forced to gang, writes through the ZIL, and reads everything
back, checking the epoch every block was born in and that its extent holds its
layout.
It then writes a last burst that only the log holds and kills itself.
The next pass imports the pool, so that the log is claimed and replayed, reads
everything from a cold cache, scrubs, damages as many disks of every block as
the block's own parity can lose and reads again, checks that the vdev does not
claim more redundancy than the lowest parity of its table, and frees the gang
blocks.
The zdb run that follows fails on leaked space.
The test needs at least four raidz disks, and
.Fl R
sets the parity of the vdev; with a parity of 2 or 3 the epoch of parity 1 also
tests the redundancy the vdev claims.
It cannot be combined with
.Fl X ,
.Fl Y
or
.Fl Z .
.It Fl Z , -raidz-parity-epochs-mix
Change the parity and the logical width of the raidz vdevs in the course of
the run.
The pool gets raidz top-level vdevs of at least four disks, and from time to
time the parity-epoch table of one of them is extended by an epoch of another
layout, which starts a few transaction groups later.
The new epoch has a random parity and, half of the time, a random logical
width narrower than the vdev, with at least one data column, so that its
blocks span several rows of that width.
Blocks then have the layout of the epoch they are born in, while the other
operations go on: gang blocks, the ZIL, scrub, resilver, replace, fault
injection, export and import, and the kill that ends a pass.
The tables stored are checked after each kill.
Fault injection stays within the lowest parity of any table, and the zdb run
that ends each pass fails if it reports leaked space.
It cannot be combined with
.Fl X ,
.Fl Y
or
.Fl W ,
and it forces
.Fl K Sy raidz
and
.Fl m Sy 0 .
.It Fl V , -verbose
Verbose (use multiple times for ever more verbosity).
.It Fl X , -raidz-expansion
Perform a dedicated raidz expansion test.
.It Fl Y , -raidz-parity-epochs Ns = Ns Sy all Ns | Ns Ar check Ns Oo , Ns Ar check Oc Ns …
Perform a dedicated test of the allocation paths of a raidz vdev whose parity
changes between epochs.
The pool gets one raidz vdev and a four entry parity-epoch table that cycles
through the three parities and returns to the base one, set with
.Fl R .
The checks to run are
.Sy all
or a comma separated list of:
.Bl -tag -compact -width "arith"
.It Sy arith
the round trip of the allocation factor;
.It Sy gang
the extent of gang headers;
.It Sy zil
dynamically sized allocations;
.It Sy io
gang blocks written in every epoch, freed, read back and checked by
.Xr zdb 8 .
.El
.El
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
To override
.Pa /tmp
as your location for block files, you can use the
.Fl f
option:
.Dl # ztest -f /
.Pp
To get an idea of what
.Nm
is actually testing try this:
.Dl # ztest -f / -VVV
.Pp
Maybe you'd like to run
.Nm ztest
for longer? To do so simply use the
.Fl T
option and specify the runlength in seconds like so:
.Dl # ztest -f / -V -T 120
.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width "ZF"
.It Ev ZFS_HOSTID Ns = Ns Em id
Use
.Em id
instead of the SPL hostid to identify this host.
Intended for use with
.Nm , but this environment variable will affect any utility which uses
libzpool, including
.Xr zpool 8 .
Since the kernel is unaware of this setting,
results with utilities other than ztest are undefined.
.It Ev ZFS_STACK_SIZE Ns = Ns Em stacksize
Limit the default stack size to
.Em stacksize
bytes for the purpose of
detecting and debugging kernel stack overflows.
This value defaults to
.Em 32K
which is double the default
.Em 16K
Linux kernel stack size.
.Pp
In practice, setting the stack size slightly higher is needed because
differences in stack usage between kernel and user space can lead to spurious
stack overflows (especially when debugging is enabled).
The specified value
will be rounded up to a floor of PTHREAD_STACK_MIN which is the minimum stack
required for a NULL procedure in user space.
.Pp
By default the stack size is limited to
.Em 256K .
.El
.
